>
> Huh? How is that better, or the current design worse?

The current design replaces an artifact that has already been generated
with an ordinary item of the artifact's base type, and continues loading
the bones level.

This is a perfectly worthwhile workaround, but it is still technically a
workaround; the bones are no longer an exact replica of the previous
game's state.  If you wanted to make sure that all bones files were
always exact representations of the previous game's state, you would
check before unlinking the bones that everything in the bones could be
used and/or created in the current game.  If not, don't unlink the bones
and just make a normal level instead.

Put more simply, "This Archaeologist couldn't possibly find bones with
the Orb of Detection in them, because in his universe the Orb of
Detection is being held by the Quest Nemesis.  So, he doesn't; instead,
he enters a normal level."

Doing this would be a slight improvement from a consistency standpoint.

> Since bones is an inter-games mechanism nothing really
> unexpected (WRT the actual game) or unbalancing happens.

And this, of course, is why it's not actually worth making the above
change if it requires anything more than a trivial amount of work; the
current workaround serves effectively to prevent the major issue, which
is: there can't be duplicates of unique artifacts.

